nce the boot_id changes. The `processState()` function retrieves the boot_id and combines it with the process start time field, ensuring that even if a PID is reused after a reboot, the combined identifier will differ.\n\nFor Windows and macOS, the approach differs slightly—they capture the process start time through platform-specific commands rather than relying on boot_id. When the reclaim logic checks if a lock is reclaimable, it compares the stored process start value against the current one, and if they differ, it treats the process as having been replaced. This means the system can detect PID reuse across reboots on Linux and identify reused PIDs on other platforms.\n\nNow looking at cross-host and NAS scenarios, the reclaim logic first checks if the lock has exceeded its maximum hold time before attempting to reclaim based on process state. This ensures locks aren't forcibly removed just because they exist on a different host—they must be genuinely stale first. The timestamp validation handles clock drift by relying on elapsed time rather than absolute time values, so even if system clocks differ between machines, the lock expiration is based on duration since acquisition, which remains consistent across the network.\n\nFor Windows tombstone operations, there's a potential issue with how the rename and removal are sequenced that could cause problems.\n\nOn the race condition between reclaim and normal release, the atomic rename of the lock directory prevents simultaneous reclaimers from both succeeding—whichever one fails to rename (because the path no longer exists) returns false and exits. This ensures only one process can complete the reclaim operation.\n\nThe `retire()` function renames the token to a tombstone, then attempts to remove the lock directory, catching any errors from the directory being already gone. This handles the case where another process might have already cleaned up. the `reclaim()` loop iterates through owner tokens, checking each one